  "style": {
    "id": "thermal-camera",
    "name": "Thermal Camera",
    "description": "A handheld thermal imager's view of ordinary objects: the picture is temperature, not light. Plain geometry (a desk, a PC tower with its side open, a monitor, a keyboard, a mug, a hand) rendered with an unlit temperature shader through an ironbow palette (black, deep purple, magenta, orange, yellow, white). Things glow because work is happening in them: heat sources switch on and approach their temperature exponentially, a conduction front creeps along heat sinks and boards with sqrt(t), exhaust air rises as a warm plume, a hand pressed on the desk leaves a print that spreads and fades, and everything slides back down the palette when the work stops. Slightly cooler at grazing angles (emissivity) so forms still read. Sensor character: soft low-resolution blur, a faint pixel grid, gentle seeded noise. Imager HUD on the overlay: centre crosshair with a spot reading, a vertical colour scale with min/max, and a SAMPLE DATA tag because every temperature is invented.",
